 \\u2014 thus, **invalid move**.\\n- **Play T5**: Part of a potential chow [T4,T5,T6], which is fully concealed. This sequence is valuable for patterns like Pure Straight or Mixed Triple Chow. Discarding T5 breaks this promising structure.\\n- **Play T4**: Same as above \\u2014 integral to the [T4,T5,T6] chow. Losing T4 weakens sequential potential.\\n- **Play T6**: Also part of [T4,T5,T6]. Same evaluation as T4/T5 \\u2014 risky to discard early.\\n- **Play F3**: West Wind is the **prevailing wind** (worth 2 points if pung/kong). There\\u2019s already a Peng on B7, and F3 could potentially form a pung later. Only one F3 discarded so far (by across player), three remain unshown \\u2014 moderate value in keeping.\\n- **Play B1**: You hold one B1; another is in your chow [B2,B3,B4], and the across player has melded [B1,B2,B3] \\u2014 meaning at least two B1s are already used. Only two B1s total unshown \\u2014 low chance of completing a set. Holding it offers little benefit.\\n\\nHowever, upon rechecking valid actions: **\\\"Play B3\\\"** is listed, but **you cannot discard a tile that is part of an existing meld unless you've just added to it or exposed it**, and here, B3 was used in a Chi to form [B2,B3,B4], which is now a fixed meld. Since B3 is **not in your current hand tiles**, it should not be playable. Similarly, check hand tiles: [T4, T5, T6, B1, B3, F1, F3, J2] \\u2014 wait, **B3 is listed in hand**, but also used in Chi? Contradiction.\\n\\nLet\\u2019s resolve:\\n- You performed \\u201cChi B3\\u201d earlier \\u2192 you claimed B3 from a discard to complete [B2,B3,B4].\\n- After forming a chow, the three tiles become **exposed meld**, not part of hand.\\n- Therefore, **B3 should not be in your current hand**.\\n- But the state says: *Your hand tiles are: [T4:1; T5:1; T6:1; B1:1; B3:1; F1:1; F3:1; J2:1]* \\u2192 this implies **B3 is still in hand**, which contradicts the meld logic.\\n\\nPossibility: Perhaps the system allows listing for clarity, but **B3 in hand is invalid** because it's in a meld.\\n\\nBut given the **valid actions include Play B3**, and the hand lists B3, we must assume either:\\n1. The game state representation is flawed, or\\n2. The chow [B2,B3,B4] uses different B3 instances? Not possible \\u2014 only 2 B3s left unshown, and across player has one B3 in [B1,B2,B3], so at most one available.\\n\\nWait: Unshown B3: 2 \\u2192 means 2 out of 4 remain unseen.\\nUsed: across player\\u2019s [B1,B2,B3] \\u2192 1 B3 used, your [B2,B3,B4] \\u2192 another B3 used \\u2192 total 2 used \\u2192 2 remaining \\u2192 okay.\\n\\nYou can have **one B3 in hand and one in meld**? No \\u2014 you only drew one B3 via Chi to form the meld. So the B3 in the chow is the same one you claimed \\u2014 cannot be duplicated.\\n\\nTherefore, **B3 should not be in your hand**.\\n\\nThus, **Play B3 is invalid** \\u2014 likely a bug in action list.\\n\\nSimilarly, **Play B1**: you have one B1 in hand, but across player already completed [B1,B2,B3], and another B1 possibly discarded or hidden \\u2014 only 2 unshown \\u2014 risky but possible.\\n\\nMost plausible safe and strategic discards are **F1** or **J2** \\u2014 both honors, less likely to complete others\\u2019 hands.\\n\\nBut consider:\\n- You have: [T4,T5,T6] \\u2192 strong potential for Pure Straight (if all same suit) \\u2192 yes, all Bamboo? No \\u2014 T = Bamboo, W = Character, B = Dots.\\n\\nClarify:\\n- T = Bamboo (\\u6761)\\n- B = Dots (\\u997c)\\nSo:\\n- Your meld [B2,B3,B4] \\u2192 Dots 2,3,4\\n- Your hand [T4,T5,T6] \\u2192 Bamboo 4,5,6 \\u2192 can form chow\\n- Another chow possibility: need 123 and 789 in same suit for Pure Straight\\n\\nCurrently, you have:\\n- Meld: [B2,B3,B4] (Dots 2,3,4) \\u2014 useful for lower sequences\\n- Meld: [B7,B7,B7] \\u2014 pung of Dots 7\\n- Hand: [T4,T5,T6] \\u2014 Bamboo 4,5,6 chow ready\\n\\u2192 If you can get [T1,T2,T3] or [T7,T8,T9], you might build toward Pure Straight (needs 123+456+789 in same suit)\\n\\nAlso, you have [B7,B7,B7] \\u2014 could go for All Pungs (6 pts), Half Flush (6 pts, if all Dots + honors), or even Full Flush if you shift.\\n\\nBut you have mixed suits.\\n\\nBest direction: **Half Flush in Dots** \\u2014 you already have two Dots melds: [B2,B3,B4] and [B7,B7,B7]. Need two more Dots melds.\\n\\nCurrent hand: T4,T5,T6 (Bamboo), B1 (Dot), plus honors.\\n\\nSo B1 is your only other Dot tile.\\n\\nIf you keep B1, aim for another Dots meld.\\n\\nBut options are limited.\\n\\nHonors: F1 (East), F3 (West), J2 (Green Dragon)\\n\\nPrevailing wind is **West (F3)** \\u2192 pung of F3 gives 2 pts.\\n\\nSeat wind is **East (F1)** \\u2192 pung gives 2 pts.\\n\\nNo one has shown F1 or F3 pungs yet.\\n\\nAcross player discarded F2, F4 not present \\u2014 F4:0, so all gone.\\n\\nF1: 4 unshown \\u2014 none discarded visibly \\u2014 wait: history shows:\\n- Across: Play F4\\n- Prev: Play F4\\n- Next: Play J1, J3, etc. \\u2014 no F1 played?\\nBut your history: no F1 discard\\nOthers: no visible F1 discard \\u2014 so 4 still out? Impossible \\u2014 max 4 copies.\\n\\nUnshown F1: 4 \\u2192 means all 4 are still not revealed \\u2014 so no one has discarded or melded any F1 \\u2192 possible.\\n\\nSame for F3: unshown 4 \\u2192 but across player played F2, prev played F4 \\u2014 but no F3 seen.\\n\\nActually, your hand has F3, and unshown says F3:4 \\u2014 contradiction.\\n\\nUnshown counts remaining in wall and not discarded.\\n\\nIf you hold one F3, and unshown says 4, that would mean 5 total \\u2014 impossible.\\n\\nAh! The \\\"Unshown tiles\\\" list likely means **number of tiles not yet drawn or discarded**, excluding those in player hands and melds.\\n\\nBut you hold one F3 \\u2192 so if unshown says F3:4, that suggests 4 still available, but only 3 others exist \\u2014 contradiction.\\n\\nUnless \\u201cunshown\\u201d includes unknown location \\u2014 i.e., not visible on table.\\n\\nStandard interpretation: \\u201cunshown\\u201d = remaining in wall + in opponents' hands, minus what\\u2019s visible in melds and discards.\\n\\nSo if **you hold one F3**, and **no F3 has been discarded or melded**, then **3 remain unshown** \\u2014 but the list says F3:4 \\u2192 error?\\n\\nList: F3:4 \\u2192 but only 4 total \\u2192 if you have one, max 3 unshown \\u2192 so must be typo or mislabel.\\n\\nLikely, the **unshown count excludes your hand**.\\n\\nSo: total F3: 4\\n- You have 1 \\u2192 so 3 others\\n- None discarded (no record), none melded \\u2192 so 3 unshown \\u2192 but list says F3:4 \\u2192 inconsistency.\\n\\nPerhaps the \\\"unshown\\\" includes all not-yet-revealed, including yours? That doesn\\u2019t make sense.\\n\\nBetter assumption: **\\\"unshown\\\" = number of tiles not yet observed in discards or melds**, regardless of who holds them.\\n\\nSo if you hold one F3, and no F3 has been discarded or melded by others, then **3 F3s are still hidden**, so unshown = 3? But list says 4.\\n\\nWait, list says: F3:4 \\u2192 but total only 4 \\u2192 so if unshown=4, none have been used \\u2192 but you have one in hand \\u2192 contradiction.\\n\\nTherefore, the **\\\"unshown\\\" counts only tiles not in any player's possession or melds**, i.e., remaining in wall.\\n\\nThen:\\n- Total F3: 4\\n- You have 1\\n- Others may have some\\n- Wall has F3:4 \\u2192 implies wall has 4 F3s? But only 4 total \\u2192 impossible.\\n\\nThis suggests the **unshown count is incorrect or misinterpreted**.\\n\\nGiven confusion, better to rely on **discard histories**.\\n\\nFrom all histories:\\n- No one has **Peng F1, F3**, or discarded them except:\\n  - Your history: no F1/F3 discard\\n  - Next: no\\n  - Across: Play F4\\n  - Previous: Play F4\\n\\u2192 So F1, F2, F3 \\u2014 no discards recorded? But F2:3 unshown, F1:4, F3:4 \\u2014 possible.\\n\\nBut you hold one F1 and one F3 \\u2192 so at least two honors in your hand.\\n\\nTotal wind tiles: 16 (4 winds \\u00d7 4 copies)\\n\\nAssume no discards of F1/F2/F3 except F4 (discarded twice), so:\\n\\n- F4: 0 unshown \\u2192 all gone\\n- F1: 4 unshown \\u2192 but you have one \\u2192 so 3 elsewhere? But 4 unshown suggests wall has 4 \\u2192 impossible.\\n\\nConclusion: **The \\\"unshown\\\" counts are absolute numbers of tiles not yet revealed to view**, i.e., still in wall or in closed hands, not displayed.\\n\\nSo if you have one F3, and unshown says F3:4, that must be wrong.\\n\\nMore likely, the **unshown count does not exclude your hand**, so it's the global remaining.\\n\\nBut then: total F3: 4\\n- You have 1\\n- Unshown: 4 \\u2192 implies 5 exist \\u2192 impossible.\\n\\nFinal assumption: **\\\"unshown\\\" means number of tiles of that type still available in the wall and opponents' hands, not counting your own hand**.\\n\\nSo:\\n- F3: 4 unshown \\u2192 means 4 F3s are not in your hand, but distributed among wall and others.\\n- But only 3 other F3s exist \\u2192 maximum unshown should be 3.\\n\\nStill contradiction.\\n\\nOnly resolution: **the \\\"unshown\\\" list includes all tiles not yet discarded or melded, including those in your hand**.\\n\\nSo:\\n- You have one F3\\n- No F3 discarded or melded by others\\n- So 4 F3s still in play \\u2192 unshown: F3:4 \\u2192 correct\\n\\nYes \\u2014 this makes sense.\\n\\nSo \\\"unshown\\\" = total number of that tile **not yet revealed on table via discard or meld**\\n\\nTherefore:\\n- F1:4 \\u2192 you have one, 3 others hidden\\n- F3:4 \\u2192 you have one, 3 others hidden\\n- F4:0 \\u2192 all four discarded or melded \\u2014 indeed, across and previous each played F4 once, and you played F4 twice? Your history: Play F4, Play F4 \\u2192 that's two, across: Play F4, previous: Play F4 \\u2192 total four \\u2192 yes, F4 all gone.\\n\\nGood.\\n\\nNow back to decision.\\n\\nYour best path:\\n- You have two Dots melds: [B2,B3,B4] and [B7,B7,B7]\\n- You have [T4,T5,T6] chow in Bamboo\\n- Honors: F1 (seat wind), F3 (prevailing wind), J2 (dragon), B1 (dot)\\n\\nTo maximize score, aim for **Half Flush** (6 pts) or **All Pungs** (6 pts) or **Mixed Straight** (8 pts).\\n\\nBut you have a chow [B2,B3,B4] \\u2014 so not all pungs.\\n\\nCan you convert to all pungs? Unlikely.\\n\\nMixed Straight: need 123, 456, 789 in three different suits.\\n\\nYou have:\\n- 456 in Bamboo: [T4,T5,T6]\\n- Could use 123 in Characters, 789 in Dots\\n\\nBut you have [B7,B7,B7] \\u2014 pung of 7, and [B2,B3,B4] \\u2014 2,3,4 \\u2014 not helpful for 789 chow.\\n\\nAnd you don't have 123.\\n\\nAlternatively, **Pure Straight**: 123+456+789 in same suit.\\n\\nClosest: Dots \\u2014 you have B2,B3,B4 (as chow), B7,B7,B7 (pung). If you could get [B5,B6] and [B8,B9], you could form [B5,B6,B7]? But B7 is pung.\\n\\nNot feasible.\\n\\nAnother idea: **Three Color Straight**? Not a standard pattern.\\n\\nBest bet: **Half Flush** (6 pts) in Dots + honors.\\n\\nYou have:\\n- Meld 1: [B2,B3,B4] (Dots)\\n- Meld 2: [B7,B7,B7] (Dots)\\n- Hand: B1 (Dot), T4,T5,T6 (Bamboo), F1,F3,J2 (honors)\\n\\nIf you can form another Dots meld, and keep honors, you might reach Half Flush.\\n\\nBut you only have B1 \\u2014 hard to build.\\n\\nAlternatively, discard honors to streamline.\\n\\nWhich honor is safest?\\n\\n- F1 (East): your seat wind (2 pts if pung). No one has shown interest. Discarding it risks giving it away, but no one has called East yet. Still, it's a wind \\u2014 always slightly dangerous.\\n- F3 (West): prevailing wind (2 pts). Across player has W2,W3,W4 (Characters), but no sign of West Wind meld. Still, could be held.\\n- J2: Green Dragon. Only one J1 and J3 discarded \\u2014 no dragon pungs seen. Relatively safe.\\n\\nJ2 is least likely to be wanted \\u2014 no dragon activity.\\n\\nF1 and F3 have pattern value.\\n\\nB1: Dot 1 \\u2014 terminal. You have no other B1s. Across player has [B1,B2,B3] \\u2014 so they had at least one B1. Possible they still have more, but unlikely.\\n\\nDiscard B1? But it's a dot \\u2014 helps Half Flush.\\n\\nT4,T5,T6: keep for now \\u2014 good chow.\\n\\nBest discard: **J2** \\u2014 least useful to others, no pattern dependency, and you're not building dragons.\\n\\nAlternatively, **F1** \\u2014 seat wind, but if you don't expect to get more, discard it.\\n\\nBut F1 might be safer to keep for chance of pung.\\n\\nJ2 is better to discard.\\n\\nAlso, **your valid actions include Play J2**.\\n\\nNow, **Play B3** is listed, but B3 is in your meld [B2,B3,B4] \\u2014 cannot discard.\\n\\nSimilarly, **Play B1** \\u2014 you have B1 in hand, so allowed.\\n\\nBut B1 is a dot \\u2014 more useful than J2.\\n\\nFinal decision: **Play J2**\\n\\nReason: J2 (Green Dragon) is least critical to your hand structure and poses minimal risk of helping opponents, as there is no evidence of dragon-based patterns being pursued. It scores no immediate fan unless paired, and you have no other dragons. Discarding it streamlines your hand toward Half Flush or Mixed Straight aspirations.\\n\\nAnswer: Play J2\"}"}
